  "answer": "For an honest concurrent use case, the supplied Manual gives the following guidance. These are statements of Manual practice, not legislative requirements.\n\n• Explain why you adopted your trade mark and when you knew about the earlier mark, addressing awareness from first use through to the application’s filing date. [TMM/Part28/3/3/6~2]\n\n• Describe the enquiries you made before starting use. If you made none, explain why. A bare assertion that your use was honest is not enough. [TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4]\n\n• Explain why you nevertheless considered your use honest despite knowing about the earlier mark. For example, address whether you believed consumers would not be confused and whether you had no intention of trading off the earlier mark’s goodwill. Give cogent reasons for that belief and supporting evidence where available—not merely the conclusion. [TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4]\n\n• Address your own state of mind directly, with the surrounding facts and circumstances. The Manual places the burden of establishing honest use on the applicant. [TMM/Part28/3/3/1~1]\n\nKnowing about the earlier mark does not, by itself, rule out honesty, but the Manual says it will ordinarily weigh strongly against a finding of honesty. Your explanation therefore needs to establish why your use was nevertheless honest. [TMM/Part28/3/3/1~4]"
